===================================================
Command line tool for parsing and plotting EIS data

Benjamin Helfrecht
benjamin.helfrecht@pnnl.gov
===================================================

File contents
-------------
- base.mplstyle
  Text file containing matplotlib specifications
  for generating the plots

- parse_EIS.py
  Parsing and plotting script

Note that `base.mplstyle` should be in the same
folder as `parse_EIS.py` if plots are desired.

Usage
-----
In order to run the script, you will need to install Python
along several external packages. 
You can install a minimally functional version of
Python on Windows through the Microsoft Store; you'll likely
want Python 3.10, the most recent release.
Once Python has been installed on your system,
the additional packages can be installed through pip.
Open the Windows command prompt and type:

    pip install numpy scipy matplotlib pandas

to install the required external packages.

For the moment, the script is purely a command line tool.
To run the script, use the command prompt to navigate
to the folder containing the script
using the `cd` command, by typing "cd" followed by the
path to the directory, e.g.,

    cd C:\this\is\the\path\to\the\folder

I think you can also just type "cd" followed by a space and then
drag the relevant folder into the command prompt window to change directory
to that folder as well. Once you have changed to the folder containing the Python
script from the command prompt, you can 
call the script from the command line as follows:

    python parse_EIS.py pos_file eis_file --plot

where "pos_file" is the filepath of the file containing the positional data
and "eis_file" is the filepath of the file containing the EIS data at each point
in the positional data. The "--plot" option specifies whether or not to generate
plots after parsing the data. The following plots are generated:

1. Real and Imaginary Z vs. Frequency
2. Impedance vs. Frequency
3. Phase vs. Frequency
4. Imaginary Z vs. Real Z
5. Positional plots of Real Z, Imaginary Z, Impedance, and Phase

You can get more information about the required and optional arguments for the script
by calling the script with the "--help" option:

    python parse_EIS.py --help

Outputs
-------
The script provides several outputs:

1. A file with the same base name as the `pos_file` and appended with "_str" (short for "stripped").
   This output contains the same data as the `pos_file`, just with the extra header lines removed.
2. A directory with the same base name as the `eis_file` and appended with "_Groups".
   This directory contains the data from the `eis_file` split into separate (numbered) files,
   with each numbered file contaning only the data corresponding to a single point from the `pos_file`.
   The numerical order of the numbered files also corresponds to the ordering of the points
   in the `pos_file`. 
3. A `Figures` directory containing the plots and their corresponding data.
   This directory is only created and populated if the "--plot" option is provided.
   For the positional plots (heatmaps), a plot is generated for each quantity
   (Real Z, Imaginary Z, Impedance, Phase) at each frequency.
   For the line plots, a plot is generated for each point from the `pos_file`
   and is also numbered according to the ordering of the points in the `pos_file`.


V22 package note: plot outputs are sample-scoped under Figures/<input_file_sample_name>/... to prevent overwriting when multiple files are parsed in the same folder.
